[{"body":"## Finding\n\nTen records whose **label explicitly names a counterion or says \"salt\"** carry a\nmapping whose **recorded structure is a net-charged ion**. Seven are graded\n`EXACT_MATCH` — the strongest identity claim available, asserting that a salt\n*is* its anion.\n\n| net charge | identifier | quality | label | term (verified OLS4) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| −2 | CHEBI:16810 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na2 alpha-ketoglutarate` | `2-oxoglutarate(2-)` |\n| −2 | CHEBI:29988 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na-glutamate` | `L-glutamate(2-)` |\n| −2 | CHEBI:30924 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na-tartrate` | `L-tartrate(2-)` |\n| −1 | CHEBI:29032 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Ca-pantothenate` | `(R)-pantothenate` |\n| −1 | CHEBI:35899 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Na-crotonate` | `crotonate` |\n| +1 | CHEBI:46020 | **EXACT_MATCH** | `Tetramethyl ammonium` | (cation) |\n| −2 | cas:12208-13-8 | FALLBACK_REGISTRY | `potassium hexahydroxoantimonate` | — |\n| −1 | cas:207511-06-6 | NARROW_MATCH | `L-Malic acid disodium salt monohydrate` | — |\n| +1 | cas:9067-32-7 | NARROW_MATCH | `Hyaluronic acid sodium salt from S. equi` | — |\n| +1 | kgmicrobe.compound:… | CLOSE_MATCH | `Tetramethyl ammonium chloride` | — |\n\nAll five CHEBI labels above verified live against OLS4; none obsolete.\n\n## Why these are not covered by the #213 convention\n\nMIM deliberately grounds **bare anion labels** (`Fumarate`, `Citrate`, `Lactate`)\nto the fully-deprotonated CHEBI class, and that is correct — a bare label carries\nno counterion, and the anion is the species present at growth-medium pH.\n\nThese ten are a different case: the label **names the counterion**\n(`Na2 …`, `Ca-…`, `… sodium salt`). Sodium glutamate and glutamate(2−) are\ndifferent substances with different formulas, masses and CAS numbers. So the\nconvention does not license `EXACT_MATCH` here, and the anion mapping — while a\nreasonable *close/narrow* relation — overstates as identity.\n\nNote also `Na-glutamate`: monosodium glutamate is the **mono**-anion salt, so\neven the charge state of the current target is off by one.\n\n## Detection\n\nMechanical and high-precision. Net charge must be computed across **both** InChI\nlayers — `/q` alone gives 119 false positives, because a neutral salt like AlCl3\nis written `/q+3;;;/p-3`:\n\n```python\nnet = sum(component /q charges) + /p          # non-zero  => recorded structure is an ion\ndefect = net != 0 and label names a counterion or \"salt\"\n```\n\nThat narrows 119 → 40 → **10**, with no false positives in the 10 on inspection.\n\n## Suggested action\n\nPer record: either re-ground to a CHEBI salt term where one exists, or keep the\nanion term and downgrade `mapping_quality` to `CLOSE_MATCH`/`NARROW_MATCH` with\nthe reason recorded. The `EXACT_MATCH` grade is the part that is indefensible.\n\nWorth adding the check to the validation suite so a future promotion sweep\ncannot reintroduce it.\n\n## Provenance\n\nSurfaced by the Claude research lane on `1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium_Lysine`\n(CHEBI:61326 is the [C2mim]⁺ cation alone; the ingredient is a neutral salt),\nthen generalised repo-wide. Related: #312 (ledger triage), #309 (the two-lane\nharness).","number":315,"state":"closed","title":"Salt-named records mapped EXACT_MATCH to the bare anion (10 records)","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/315"},{"body":"## The check\n\nFlag any record whose `preferred_term` names a hydrate (`x n H2O`, `·nH2O`,\n`hydrate`) while its recorded formula/SMILES/InChI contain **no water\ncomponent**. Purely mechanical.\n\n**Result: 26 records.**\n\n## Why the existing CAS-vs-structure check cannot see this\n\nBoth compared fields are populated from the same ChEBI term by\n`AUTO_BACKFILL_CHEBI_CHEMISTRY`, so they agree **by construction**. The check\ncompares the record against itself; the *label* is the only field carrying the\ncontradicting information, and nothing was comparing against it.\n\n## The sharper pattern: one term absorbing many distinct hydrates\n\n**10 ontology terms carry 26 distinct hydrate labels between them:**\n\n| term | absorbs |\n|---|---|\n| CHEBI:34683 | `Na2HPO4 x 2/3/6/7/12 H2O` — **five** different hydrates |\n| CHEBI:35696 | `CoCl2 x 2/4/6 H2O` |\n| CHEBI:34887 | `NiCl2 x 2/5/6 H2O` |\n| CHEBI:75832 | `FeSO4 x 5/6/7 H2O` |\n| CHEBI:23414 | `CuSO4 x 2/4 H2O` |\n| CHEBI:30812 | `FeCl2 x 6/7 H2O` |\n| CHEBI:86360 | `MnSO4 x 1/7 H2O` |\n| CHEBI:53001 | `NiSO4 x 6/7 H2O` |\n\nDifferent hydrates are different substances with different formula weights and\ndifferent CAS numbers. Collapsed onto one term they are indistinguishable\ndownstream — a recipe calling for the heptahydrate and one calling for the\ndihydrate become the same node.\n\n**The assignment is demonstrably arbitrary.** Magnesium sulfate splits across two\nterms *by notation rather than by chemistry*:\n\n```\nCHEBI:32599  <- \"MgSO4 x 6 H2O\",  \"MgSO4·7H2O\"\nCHEBI:31795  <- \"MgSO4 x 7 H2O\",  \"MgSO4·H2O\"\n```\n\nThe **same** hydration number (7) lands on two different terms depending on\nwhether the label was written `x 7 H2O` or `·7H2O`. That is lexical matching\nwith no chemistry behind it.\n\nOne record states the contradiction outright: `Na2-EDTA x 2 H2O` carries\n`ontology_label: EDTA disodium salt (anhydrous)`.\n\n## Triage forks three ways — do not mass-apply\n\nEstablished from three worked cases:\n\n1. **The label is wrong.** `CaSO4 x 7 H2O` — no calcium sulfate heptahydrate\n   exists (ChEBI has anhydrous/hemi/dihydrate only). Probably a `2`→`7` slip,\n   but confirming needs the source recipes read. MediaDive maps\n   `CaSO4 x 2 H2O` correctly, so it does distinguish these when they are real.\n2. **The label is right and the ontology is deficient.** `CeCl3 x 7 H2O` is the\n   standard commercial form; ChEBI has **no** cerium chloride hydrate at all.\n   Ignoring the waters here is a **51% formula-weight error** (246 → 373).\n   Candidate for a registry term.\n3. **Neither side is definite.** `Cefsulodin sodium salt hydrate` — no hydrate\n   term in any ontology *and* the label states no hydration number. Nothing to\n   encode; the fix is dropping `EXACT_MATCH` and recording why.\n\nSo a lexical `x n H2O` sweep would fix (1) and corrupt (2).\n\n## Suggested action\n\nAdd the detector as a standing check, then triage the 26 by the fork above.\nThe `Na2HPO4` five-way and `CoCl2`/`NiCl2` three-way collapses are the highest\nvalue — they are the cases where distinct media ingredients have become\nindistinguishable.\n\n## Provenance\n\nDetector proposed by the Claude research lane after the CAS-vs-structure check\npassed on three defective records in one batch, with the correct diagnosis of\nwhy it structurally could not catch them. Verified repo-wide.\nRelated: #315, #317, #320.","number":321,"state":"closed","title":"26 hydrate-named records map to anhydrous terms; 10 terms absorb multiple distinct hydrates","url":"https://github.com/CultureBotAI/MediaIngredientMech/issues/321"}]
